Title:
Robots, Teams, and Cooperative Perception: From Exploration to Videography

Abstract:

Over the last ten years, drones have become increasingly integrated into our
society, industry, and media.
Most prominently, drones are being used to carry sensors and cameras to film,
observer, and inspect the world around us.
As this process goes on, we will see autonomy continue to improve and greater
numbers of drones involved in more varied and complex tasks.

For autonomous drones, processes of navigating and observing unknown objects and
environments are pervasive in across mapping, tracking, inspection, and
cinematography.
Autonomy is also important to application domains such as search and
rescue where autonomous operation is critical to enabling mobile robots to
penetrate through rubble outside of communication range with human operators.
This talk will be split into two parts:
the first part will focus on robots exploring unknown environments and
applications to problems like search and rescue;
and the latter part will introduce my work on multi-robot perception planning
my ongoing work in the AirLab on multidrone
reconstruction where we are working on filming groups of people in the wild with
exciting implications spanning from team sports to the study of animal groups.

Active perception tasks such as autonomously mapping a building or tracking
groups of targets often lead to optimization problems that are difficult to
solve (e.g. NP-Hard) and yet highly structured.
Taking advantage of structure can reduce computation costs and simplify these
problems such as by enabling efficient and accurate objective evaluation or
development of distributed algorithms with strong suboptimality guarantees.
In particular, I will present my work developing the first submodular
optimization algorithms for a large class of perception planning problems that
scale to large numbers of robots while running with a constant number of
communication rounds.
The methods I will discuss enable individual robots to navigate and observe
unknown environments at high speeds and enable teams to quickly and collectively
react to information from new observations.

Since starting at the AirLab, I have been working to develop teams of flying
cameras that coordinate to observe interactions between groups of people and
record video to produce a dense 3D reconstructions of those groups their motion.
Tracking those people in real time, planning out view points, safely
coordinating the multi-robot team, and reconstructing scenes are all challenging
problems that our team is working to solve.
I will briefly describe the system we are building before discussing role of
multi-robot coordination in this domain.
